WEBVTT
00:00:01.500 --> 00:01:02.360
<v Ira Glass>There's what we wish for and there's what we get. For Susan Bergman, the story went like this. Her father led a double life. On the one hand, he proudly described himself as a family man, a church organist, in a denomination that was so strict the women covered their heads, wore no make-up, no dancing, no smoking, no drinking, no going to theaters, no swimming with members of the opposite sex, even. Her father stage-managed things so they appeared to be the perfect blond-haired, blue-eyed, American family. But secretly, he was having sex with men. By all accounts, it was lots of men. Sometimes he would even fly off to New York, go to gay clubs there.
<v Ira Glass>生活中有我们期盼的事，也有我们最终得到的事。对苏珊·伯格曼来说，故事是这样的：她的父亲过着双重生活。一方面，他引以为豪地称自己是一个顾家的男人、一名教会风琴手，身处一个极为保守的教派中——那里的女性要遮住头发、不化妆、不跳舞、不吸烟、不喝酒，甚至连看戏和与异性一同游泳都是被禁止的。她的父亲一手操办了一切，使他们看起来是一个金发碧眼、完美的美国中产家庭。但私下里，他却在与男性发生性关系。据各方说法，对象还不少。有时他甚至会飞到纽约，去那里的同性恋俱乐部。

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:41.080
<v Ira Glass>In 1983, he was one of the first victims of AIDS. They had barely named the disease. The symptoms weren't familiar. And he died before his children got the chance to ask him about who he really was and talk to him about how they should reconcile who he had pretended to be all those years, with who he was. What was real of their childhood?
<v Ira Glass>1983年，他成了最早期的艾滋病受害者之一。当时人们几乎还没给这种病命名，症状也不为人所知。他在孩子们有机会询问他究竟是谁，并与他谈论如何调和多年来他扮演的角色与真实自我之间的矛盾之前，就去世了。他们童年里的哪些部分是真实的呢？

00:01:41.080 --> 00:02:18.810
<v Ira Glass>Susan Bergman wrote a book about her family's experience. And on her book tour, a very particular thing started to happen. Gay men, who were still married or who had been married, started to contact her. They wanted to explain her father's double life to her. They wanted to explain their own double lives to her. And they wanted to offer her the conversation that she never got to have with her father.
<v Ira Glass>苏珊·伯格曼写了一本关于她家庭经历的书。在她的新书巡回宣传中，一件非常特别的事情发生了。那些仍然维持婚姻或曾经结过婚的男同性恋者开始联系她。他们想向她解释她父亲的双重生活，想向她解释他们自己的双重生活。他们想提供给她那种她从未能与父亲进行的对话。

00:02:18.810 --> 00:03:30.650
<v Ira Glass>Well, from WBEZ in Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Back once again for another hour documenting life in these United States. Today's program in four acts. Act One, gay men. I talk to Susan Bergman about her father. Act Two, a gay man talks to me in a parked car in an undisclosed location about why he thinks that his children don't need to know that he's gay and why he stays married. Act Three, we take all the heavy, very heavy themes about lying and families from the first two acts and rework them as comedy, if you can believe that. And finally, Act Four, the sins of the fathers pass on to the sons. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台的《美国生活》(This American Life) 节目，我是艾拉·格拉斯。再次回归，为你记录这片美国大地上的生活。今天的节目分为四幕。第一幕，男同性恋者。我与苏珊·伯格曼谈论她的父亲。第二幕，一位男同性恋者在未公开地点的停放车辆中，与我探讨为什么他认为自己的孩子不需要知道他是同性恋，以及为什么他选择维持婚姻。第三幕，我们将前两幕中关于谎言和家庭的沉重主题进行重构，将其转化为喜剧，如果你敢相信的话。最后，第四幕，父辈的罪孽传递给子辈。请继续收听。

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:38.960
<v Ira Glass>Act One, The Book Tour. Susan Bergman came to our studios, loaded down with evidence.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，《新书巡回》。苏珊·伯格曼带着满载的证据来到了我们的演播室。

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:52.640
<v Ira Glass>OK, now you've brought in some tapes of yourself being interviewed on talk radio shows that I've got here. So this first one, do we need to explain it at all before we play it?
<v Ira Glass>好的，现在你带了一些你在谈话广播节目中接受采访的录音带，我这儿都有。那么第一段录音，在播放之前我们需要解释一下背景吗？

00:03:52.640 --> 00:04:01.330
<v Susan Bergman>I think this was a caller that was, in some ways, typical of the response that I got on talk radio.
<v Susan Bergman>我认为这是一个听众打来的电话，他在某种程度上代表了我在谈话广播中收到的典型反馈。

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:02.750
<v Ira Glass>Here we go.
<v Ira Glass>开始了。

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:53.850
<v Man>I have a particularly close connection to this sort of thing. I'm a gay father, divorced now. So I'm very close to this. In particular, I can understand your father's secretiveness and his pain. There's always the resentment that you had to go through the pain, that you had to hide, that you had to lie. And you live with that resentment. It really never quite goes away, even in my case, after you've come completely out and divorced and the whole bit. And the resentment lives on, that I had to do live that lie, that I had to lie to my wife, that I had to lie to my children, that I had to life to my family. And never be happy. Never, never, never know happiness.
<v Man>我和这种事情有着特别紧密的联系。我是一位男同性恋父亲，现在已经离婚了。所以我对此感同身受。特别是，我能理解你父亲的隐秘和痛苦。总是会有一种怨恨，怨恨自己不得不经历那种痛苦，不得不隐藏，不得不撒谎。你带着那种怨恨生活。它真的从未完全消失，即使像我这样已经完全出柜并离婚，经历了这一切之后。那种怨恨依然存在：我不得不活在那样的谎言中，不得不对我的妻子撒谎，不得不对我的孩子们撒谎，不得不对我的家庭撒谎。而且永远无法快乐。永远，永远，永远无法知晓幸福。

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:54.620
<v Ira Glass>Hey, Susan?
<v Ira Glass>嘿，苏珊？

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:55.470
<v Susan Bergman>Uh-huh.
<v Susan Bergman>嗯。

00:04:55.470 --> 00:05:10.560
<v Ira Glass>Can I just ask you, what was your reaction to-- when he was saying this, had it occurred to you before that your father would actually be resentful of the family for the secret that he was keeping from you?
<v Ira Glass>我能问问你的反应吗——当他说这些话时，你以前是否想到过，你的父亲实际上会因为他向你隐瞒的秘密而对家庭感到怨恨？

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:42.710
<v Susan Bergman>He was angry at us a lot. But it seemed like it was more-- I mean I interpreted that as we were bothering Dad, or Mom was asking too many questions. Yeah. It's not one that had occurred to me. And I would have to contest that assumption that one has to lie or one is put in a situation where lying is the only appropriate gesture or response.
<v Susan Bergman>他经常对我们发火。但我过去以为那是……我的意思是，我解读为我们在打扰爸爸，或者妈妈问了太多问题。是的，这个念头我从未有过。而且我不得不反驳那种认为“一个人必须撒谎”或者“一个人身处谎言是唯一合适的姿态或回应”的假设。

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:44.230
<v Ira Glass>Let's go back to this tape.
<v Ira Glass>让我们回到录音。

00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:54.500
<v Man>And I hope that-- I don't know. Is there anything that I can help you. Any questions that I could answer. Because I'm still alive and I've been through it.
<v Man>我希望……我不知道。有什么我能帮你的吗？有什么我可以回答的问题吗？因为我还活着，而且我经历过这一切。

00:05:54.500 --> 00:06:09.550
<v Susan Bergman>That's great. Boy, I hope I can do this without just kind of gushing all over you, because it's so great of you to call in and offer that. It's beautiful. I didn't get that chance with my father. I guess I would ask you--
<v Susan Bergman>太棒了。天哪，我希望我能不至于在你面前表现得情绪失控，因为你能打来电话并提出这些帮助真是太好了。这很美好。我没有机会与我的父亲进行这样的对话。我想我会问你……

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:18.610
<v Ira Glass>I'm just stopping the tape here. I'm just going to stop the tape here, because as you're listening, you're making the gagging signal at your own reaction.
<v Ira Glass>我在这里暂停录音。我只是要把录音停在这里，因为当你听的时候，你对自己听到的内容做出了呕吐的手势。

00:06:18.610 --> 00:07:04.990
<v Susan Bergman>Oh, I think that's so great. Oh, I think that's so wonderful. I was not in touch with the full range of the reaction until after I hung up the telephone and stopped the radio interview. And clearly, that man was hurting in a lot of ways. And the last thing I needed to do was have a fight with him on the air about being a liar, or whatever. Whatever anger I would have had towards my father didn't seem to me to apply to him. He was just some innocent bystander. So I just tried to be nice. And it was kind-- oh yeah.
<v Susan Bergman>噢，我觉得那太好了。噢，我觉得那太精彩了。在挂掉电话并结束广播采访之前，我并没有意识到自己反应的全貌。很显然，那个男人在很多方面都很痛苦。我最不需要做的就是在直播中为了“他是骗子”之类的事情和他争吵。无论我对父亲有什么愤怒，似乎都无法适用于他。他只是一个无辜的旁观者。所以我尽量保持礼貌。那是一种……噢，是的。

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:57.030
<v Ira Glass>There's the thing that you wish for and there's the thing that you get. And if what you really need is a long talk with your father, the kind of talk where you get mad and argue, and maybe he gets mad, and maybe people admit mistakes, and maybe things get resolved or maybe they don't get resolved, and you learn that they won't get resolved. But if that conversation is what you need, then no stranger on a radio call-in show, however well meaning, is going to give that to you. It's a mockery of what you need.
<v Ira Glass>生活中有你期盼的事，也有你最终得到的事。如果你真正需要的是与父亲进行一场深谈——那种你会生气并争论，或许他也会生气，或许人们会承认错误，或许事情得到解决，又或许事情无法解决，而你学会了它们终将无法解决的谈话——那么广播访谈节目中任何陌生人，无论出于多好的意愿，都无法给予你这些。这只是对你所渴望的东西的一种嘲弄。

00:07:57.030 --> 00:08:16.400
<v Ira Glass>But there you are. There you are. You're talking to this man, this stranger. And you find yourself asking a version of the question that you would most like to ask your own father. And you get an answer that is totally useless, because really it has nothing to do with you.
<v Ira Glass>但你就在那里。你就在那里。你在和这个男人，这个陌生人交谈。你发现自己在问一个你最想问自己父亲的问题的版本。然后你得到了一个完全无用的回答，因为说到底，这和你自己的父亲毫无关系。

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:30.440
<v Susan Bergman>You said, you didn't want to share your quote, "real life," maybe. But why do you feel that you were forced to lie about that? How could your family have shared your life?
<v Susan Bergman>你说过，你不想分享你所谓的“真实生活”，或许吧。但为什么你觉得你被迫对此撒谎？你的家庭原本可以如何分享你的生活？

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:39.760
<v Man>You see the problem is that once you get trapped into doing the thing you think you're supposed to do at a young age-- I married at 23 because I thought it was-- I knew I was gay then already.
<v Man>你看，问题在于，一旦你在年轻时就被困在做你认为“应该做”的事情里——我在23岁时结婚，因为我以为那是……我那时已经知道自己是同性恋了。

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:40.010
<v Susan Bergman>Hm.
<v Susan Bergman>嗯。

00:08:40.010 --> 00:08:51.680
<v Man>So did my wife, for that matter. She put it aside.
<v Man>我妻子也知道。她只是把它搁置了。

00:08:51.680 --> 00:09:34.940
<v Ira Glass>Gay men sent Susan Bergman photos of themselves. One happy couple stands there in one of the photos in matching sweaters, raising glasses, smiling. The letter said that if her father had lived and divorced her mother, this is the kind of happiness he could have had. The letters that Susan Bergman got from the children and spouses of gay fathers, she said, seemed to be written in a completely different language than the letters she got from the gay fathers. It was like two different cultures, two different perspectives.
<v Ira Glass>男同性恋者给苏珊·伯格曼寄来了他们的照片。其中一张照片里，一对幸福的伴侣穿着配套的毛衣，举杯微笑。信中说，如果她的父亲还活着并与母亲离婚，他本可以拥有这种幸福。苏珊·伯格曼从那些男同性恋父亲的子女和配偶那里收到的信件，她说，似乎是用一种完全不同的语言写成的，与她从那些男同性恋父亲那里收到的信完全不同。就像两种不同的文化、两种不同的视角。

00:09:34.940 --> 00:10:02.210
<v Ira Glass>There are lots of books about men coming out, gay men coming out, gay women coming out. But nobody seemed to be able to remember a book that had been told from the point of view of the children and the wives that these gay men had left behind. And families who are in that situation felt this shock of we are not alone. And they contacted her.
<v Ira Glass>有很多关于男人出柜、男同性恋者出柜、女同性恋者出柜的书。但似乎没人能想起有一本书是从这些被遗弃的子女和妻子的角度来讲述的。那些处于这种情况下的家庭感受到了这种震惊：我们并不孤单。于是他们联系了她。

00:10:02.210 --> 00:10:05.120
<v Ira Glass>You brought examples of letters?
<v Ira Glass>你带来了信件的例子吗？

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:37.750
<v Susan Bergman>Yeah. The most poignant letter that I got, this woman says she read the book. "Our lives are so similar, Susan, that it was eerie for me to read." She talks about their background. They were all so fundamentalist. Our family wasn't quite fundamentalist. But this woman says, "Bible believing churches that started in their living room. We had six well-behaved, talented, athletic children in our prosperous and highly visible family."
<v Susan Bergman>是的。我收到的最令人心酸的信件里，这位女士说她读了这本书。“我们的生活太相似了，苏珊，读的时候我觉得毛骨悚然。”她谈到了他们的背景。他们都非常原教旨主义。我们的家庭并不完全是原教旨主义的。但这位女士说，“在客厅里发起的信奉圣经的教会。在我们富有且备受瞩目的家庭里，我们有六个乖巧、有天赋、热爱运动的孩子。”

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:59.770
<v Susan Bergman>"When my dad was diagnosed in 1988," she writes, "my mom kicked him out of the house and would have nothing else to do with him. Three of us moved him out of town, encouraged him to change his name, and lied about his mysterious disease and our parent's sudden separation. Now, they are both gone. And we are left to deal with the fraud that was our life."
<v Susan Bergman>“1988年我父亲被确诊时，”她写道，“我母亲把他赶出了家门，对他不理不睬。我们三个孩子把他搬到了外地，鼓励他改名换姓，并对他那神秘的疾病和父母的突然分居撒了谎。现在，他们俩都走了。我们只能去处理那些曾经构成我们生活的谎言。”

00:10:59.770 --> 00:11:27.920
<v Susan Bergman>They had just buried their mother-- when she wrote me this letter-- who had died of AIDS because the father didn't protect the mother at all. She says, "I'm writing to you now, not to pour out my heart, but to ask if my sisters and I can come take you out for lunch soon there in Chicago? There are not many like us who have suffered such circumstances. And I know in my heart that I should be able to hold my head high and talk about my mother's needless death without shame. But as of now, I can't."
<v Susan Bergman>当她写这封信给我时，他们刚刚埋葬了母亲——她死于艾滋病，因为父亲根本没有保护过母亲。她说，“我写信给你，不是为了倾诉心声，而是想问问我的姐妹们和我是否能在芝加哥和你共进午餐？像我们这样遭受过这种情况的人不多。我在心里知道，我应该昂首挺胸，毫无羞愧地谈论母亲那不必要的死亡。但目前，我还做不到。”

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:36.740
<v Susan Bergman>That's the end of that letter. And I talked to this family at length, on several different occasions. They did come to Chicago.
<v Susan Bergman>那封信到这里就结束了。我在好几次不同的场合与这个家庭进行了深入交谈。他们确实来了芝加哥。

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:37.430
<v Ira Glass>They did?
<v Ira Glass>他们真的来了？

00:11:37.430 --> 00:11:51.260
<v Susan Bergman>Yeah. Two of the daughters came. Some of the sons were not willing to even acknowledge that the parents had died of AIDS yet, one of them being a physician.
<v Susan Bergman>是的。其中两个女儿来了。有些儿子甚至不愿承认父母是死于艾滋病，其中一个还是医生。

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:52.430
<v Ira Glass>One of the sons being a physician?
<v Ira Glass>其中一个儿子是医生？

00:11:52.430 --> 00:12:30.000
<v Susan Bergman>Yeah. But the interesting story that they have, as her father was dying, they spent hours and hours interviewing their father on tape about his sexual contacts. They got the names of all of the married men that he had been with in this three-city area and the addresses and the phone numbers, which he had. And they began calling those men's wives on the telephone, because they wanted to save lives, because they knew their mother was dying.
<v Susan Bergman>是的。但他们有一个有趣的故事。在父亲弥留之际，他们花了数小时在录音带上采访父亲关于他性接触的情况。他们得到了他在这个三城市地区接触过的所有已婚男性的名单，还有地址和电话号码，这些都是他留下的。他们开始给那些男人的妻子打电话，因为他们想挽救生命，因为他们知道自己的母亲快要死了。

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:33.350
<v Ira Glass>And so they would call up these women and say, you don't know me but--
<v Ira Glass>所以他们会打电话给这些女人说，你不认识我，但是——

00:12:33.350 --> 00:12:34.150
<v Susan Bergman>Yup.
<v Susan Bergman>是的。

00:12:34.150 --> 00:12:36.610
<v Ira Glass>--my father has had sex with your husband.
<v Ira Glass>——我父亲和你丈夫发生过性关系。

00:12:36.610 --> 00:13:15.290
<v Susan Bergman>Yes. I mean can you imagine that? I mean, of course they debated this among themselves. They thought this is none of our business. But when their mother got AIDS, they knew that this list of 100 married men were not taking any precautions with their own wives. And they started calling. And I said, "Well, what was the reaction?" I asked them to go on. And they said a lot of women hung up the phone them. And some would say, "Oh, thank you very much for calling."
<v Susan Bergman>是的。我的意思是，你能想象吗？当然，他们自己在内部也争论过。他们认为这不关我们的事。但当他们的母亲感染艾滋病后，他们知道名单上的那100个已婚男人在与自己的妻子相处时并没有采取任何预防措施。于是他们开始打电话。我问他们：“那么，她们的反应是什么？”他们说，很多女人挂断了电话。也有一些人会说：“噢，非常感谢你的来电。”

00:13:15.290 --> 00:13:27.990
<v Ira Glass>And as you say that, the thing that occurs to me is it's such a complicated act, because partly it's an act of compassion for somebody. And then partly it's such an act of vengeance against somebody else, and calling--
<v Ira Glass>当你这样说时，我想到的是，这是一种极其复杂的行为。因为在某种程度上，它是对某人的怜悯，而在另一方面，它又是对另一方的复仇，揭露——

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:28.400
<v Susan Bergman>I know but--
<v Susan Bergman>我知道，但是——

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:29.520
<v Ira Glass>--out that man.
<v Ira Glass>——那个男人的真面目。

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:36.070
<v Susan Bergman>I mean if you lose your mother to AIDS, I can see why. I never had to do that.
<v Susan Bergman>我的意思是，如果你因艾滋病失去了母亲，我能理解为什么。我从没经历过那种事。

00:13:36.070 --> 00:13:36.530
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:13:36.530 --> 00:14:15.800
<v Susan Bergman>But I'll tell you, in the town I live in, outside Chicago, there is a family, at least one, where the same thing is going on. The father is very promiscuous, a prominent man in town, has four young children. His wife has no idea he's a practicing homosexual. They have unprotected sex. And people in the community come to me and say, "What should we do? We've asked him to tell her. We're thinking about telling her. We want to protect her so that her kids have one parent." And when I talk to--
<v Susan Bergman>但我得告诉你，在我居住的芝加哥郊外的一个小镇，有一个家庭，至少有一个，正在发生同样的事。那个父亲非常滥交，是镇上的知名人物，有四个年幼的孩子。他的妻子根本不知道他是一个活跃的同性恋者。他们有不安全的性行为。社区里的人来找我说：“我们该怎么办？我们让他告诉她。我们正在考虑要不要告诉她。我们想保护她，这样她的孩子们至少能有一个健康的父母。”当我与——

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:18.820
<v Ira Glass>And what do you tell them?
<v Ira Glass>那你告诉他们什么？

00:14:18.820 --> 00:15:03.880
<v Susan Bergman>I say that's their decision. They're going to have to really think about that, because I don't know the people. I can't go into their lives just because I wrote some book on some related subject. If they're involved in the family system in some way, they have to make that decision. I have no idea. But when I talk to gay men about this book, who have read it and want to have a conversation, almost everyone has said to me, "Oh yeah, my main partners are married men." I said, "Well, you have to think about your own responsibility to the family then, I imagine."
<v Susan Bergman>我说那是他们的决定。他们必须真正考虑清楚，因为我不认识这些人。我不能因为我写了一本相关的书就闯入他们的生活。如果他们以某种方式卷入了那个家庭系统，他们就必须做出那个决定。我不知道。但当我与读过这本书并想进行交流的男同性恋者谈话时，几乎每个人都对我说：“噢，是的，我的主要伴侣都是已婚男人。”我说：“嗯，我想那你得考虑一下你对那些家庭应尽的责任了。”

00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:16.920
<v Ira Glass>All these different fathers who contacted you, did any of them say, I'm sorry, I did something that hurt people and I'm sorry?
<v Ira Glass>所有联系你的那些不同父亲，有没有人说，对不起，我做了一些伤害别人的事，我很抱歉？

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:29.790
<v Susan Bergman>Well, no. Because there's a lot of talk trying to defend the position that's just newly being articulated in their lives. And I understand that they're building their ramparts or whatever.
<v Susan Bergman>嗯，没有。因为有大量的谈话都在试图捍卫他们生活中刚刚被阐明的立场。我理解他们正在修筑防御工事之类的东西。

00:15:29.790 --> 00:15:33.500
<v Ira Glass>That position being, I was right to at least clear up this lie.
<v Ira Glass>那个立场就是：至少我有权揭开这个谎言，我是对的。

00:15:33.500 --> 00:15:46.340
<v Susan Bergman>Finally, I did the one right thing. I left my family and became a true homosexual. That's the right thing, that's being defended in almost all of the letters.
<v Susan Bergman>“最后，我做了一件正确的事。我离开了我的家庭，成为了一名真正的同性恋者。”这是对的，这几乎是所有信件中都在捍卫的事情。

00:15:46.340 --> 00:16:09.640
<v Susan Bergman>This is a very interesting letter signed, anonymously yours. He says, "At the risk of intrusion, which is not my intention, I'm compelled to write you and express some gentle viewpoints based on experiences similar to those in your book, but admittedly little real knowledge of your life." I appreciated that acknowledgement very much.
<v Susan Bergman>这是一封非常有意思的信，署名是“匿名”。他说：“冒昧打扰，并非我的本意，但我迫切地写信给你，表达一些温和的观点。这些基于与你书中相似的经历，尽管我确实对你的生活知之甚少。”我非常感激这种承认。

00:16:09.640 --> 00:16:59.070
<v Susan Bergman>"Based on your writing, however, I strongly feel that your father's life with you and your family was not the sham it may superficially appear. I see the story from a different perspective, as that of a tragic, often unconscious struggle by your father to love his family and not end up as one of nature's mistakes, which of course he wasn't. Were there no kisses of bruised knees, soothing of tears and hurt feelings, umpteen occasions of personal denial, dreams of success and happiness for you and the others? Would it be unreasonable to consider yourself doubly-loved, by a fractured psyche fighting desperately against the nature he was given? In retrospect, are not his intents as important as his failures?"
<v Susan Bergman>“然而，根据你的写作，我强烈地感觉到，你父亲与你和你家人一起的生活，并不像表面上看起来的那样是一场虚构。我从不同的视角看待这个故事，认为这只是一个悲剧性的、常常是无意识的斗争：你的父亲试图爱他的家庭，且不让自己沦为‘自然的错误’——当然他不是。难道没有亲吻擦伤的膝盖、抚慰眼泪和受伤的感情，无数次个人否定的场合，对你和其他人成功与幸福的梦想吗？考虑自己被双重爱着，被一个正在与他被赋予的天性进行绝望斗争的破碎心灵所爱，难道是不合理的吗？回想起来，他的意图难道不比他的失败更重要吗？”

00:16:59.070 --> 00:17:46.100
<v Susan Bergman>It's a beautiful letter in some ways. And in other ways, there are these irritants in these approaches. I shouldn't be so critical I suppose, but then to say to me, were there no kisses of bruised knees? Yes, of course. Yeah, I had a great Daddy in many regards. Yes, he was a split person. And I don't think that he enjoyed being split, and neither does this gentleman. But that doesn't make him a better father. And that's almost the thing that this letter is driving towards, like couldn't you be doubly loved, being loved by a homosexual father? No.
<v Susan Bergman>这在某些方面是一封美丽的信。但在另一些方面，这些做法里有一些令人恼火的东西。我不该这么挑剔，但若问我“难道没有亲吻擦伤的膝盖吗？”是的，当然有。是的，他在许多方面是一个伟大的爸爸。是的，他是一个分裂的人。我不认为他享受这种分裂，那位先生也不认为。但这并不让他成为一个更好的父亲。那封信几乎是朝着这个方向驱动的，仿佛在说：难道被一个同性恋父亲爱着，你就没有得到双倍的爱吗？不。

00:17:46.100 --> 00:18:25.980
<v Susan Bergman>I think I was well-loved by my father. I think my father was a split person, and that that destroyed him and it worked towards the destruction of his family. By coming to understand that your family was structured around a lie, that who you were told you were and that the family was, has nothing to do with what you really are or were, that's very complicated.
<v Susan Bergman>我想我确实被我的父亲深深爱着。我认为我的父亲是一个分裂的人，这摧毁了他，也导致了他家庭的破碎。通过理解你的家庭是围绕着一个谎言构建的，理解你被告知你是谁，被告知这个家庭是什么，与你真正是什么或曾是什么毫无关系，那真是非常复杂。

00:18:25.980 --> 00:20:55.360
<v Ira Glass>Susan Bergman. Her book is called Anonymity. Coming up, a gay father explains why he chooses not to tell his children of his double life.
<v Ira Glass>苏珊·伯格曼。她的书名为《匿名》(Anonymity)。接下来，一位男同性恋父亲解释了他为什么选择不告诉孩子们他的双重生活。

00:20:55.360 --> 00:21:11.020
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, Dad in the Closet. This next story isn't intended to answer whatever questions Susan Bergman might have about her father. It's intended to answer our questions about these gay men who stay in their marriages, leading double lives.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，柜子里的爸爸。接下来的这个故事并非旨在回答苏珊·伯格曼可能对自己父亲的任何疑问。它旨在回答我们关于那些留在婚姻中、过着双重生活的男同性恋者的问题。

00:21:11.020 --> 00:21:40.370
<v Ira Glass>The man we found to interview for this has been married for 26 years, heads a support group, called Review, for men in similar circumstances. He was willing to be interviewed, but he didn't want me coming to his home and he didn't want to come to our studios either. On the phone, I understood this to mean that he didn't feel comfortable appearing in public like this. Later, he told me that I had misunderstood, that he just didn't want to drive that far. In any case, we met in a parking lot on Damen Avenue and drove to a quiet street, where I conducted the interview, in his car.
<v Ira Glass>我们找到采访的这位男士已经结婚26年，负责一个名为“回顾”(Review)的互助小组，专门针对处于类似情况的男性。他愿意接受采访，但他不希望我去他的家，也不想来我们的演播室。在电话里，我理解为他不想以这种方式在公众面前露面。后来他告诉我，是我误解了，他只是不想开那么远的车。总之，我们在达门大道(Damen Avenue)的一个停车场见面，然后开车到了一条安静的街道上，我在他的车里进行了采访。

00:21:40.370 --> 00:21:42.680
<v Jerry Walters>My name is Jerry Walters.
<v Jerry Walters>我叫杰里·沃尔特斯。

00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:45.160
<v Ira Glass>Now let me just ask you, is it OK for us to use your name on the radio?
<v Ira Glass>现在让我问一下，我们在广播中使用你的名字可以吗？

00:21:45.160 --> 00:22:02.360
<v Jerry Walters>Jerry Walters isn't my real name. It's a name that when I used to work on a hotline, they said pick out a name that you're comfortable with and use that. And so that's what I've been using. Plus, it separates business from my club activities. So Jerry Walters is fine.
<v Jerry Walters>杰里·沃尔特斯不是我的真名。以前我在热线工作时，他们说选一个你觉得舒服的名字用。所以我就一直用这个。而且，这把生意和我的俱乐部活动区分开了。所以叫杰里·沃尔特斯没问题。

00:22:02.360 --> 00:22:26.700
<v Ira Glass>This man is in his fifties, was a teenager during the Eisenhower years. He looked like any suburban dad. He was neatly dressed, in gray wool slacks, a sweater, and what appeared to be a clip-on necktie. He says he was always a good boy. He says he doesn't really get at angry people, doesn't know how to yell at people. Back in high school, he says, he was the kind of boy who'd go out with girls, but never make passes at them.
<v Ira Glass>这个男人五十多岁，在艾森豪威尔时期度过了青少年时代。他看起来就像任何一个郊区老爸。他衣着整洁，穿着灰色羊毛长裤、一件毛衣，还有看起来像是夹上去的领带。他说他一直是个好孩子。他说他真的不会对人生气，不知道怎么对人咆哮。回想起高中时，他说他是那种会和女孩约会，但从不跟她们调情的那种男孩。

00:22:26.700 --> 00:22:56.900
<v Jerry Walters>I graduated. I would date occasionally. And I did find somebody that seemed like a very nice person, that we had a lot in common. And we went out on a date. But that afternoon, after I met her in the morning, and that afternoon I had my first gay experience. And so, it was really kind of a red-letter day. I went bowling with her in the evening. And I was out with another man that afternoon.
<v Jerry Walters>我毕业了。我偶尔会约会。我确实找到了一个看起来很不错的人，我们有很多共同点。我们约会过。但在那次约会的下午——那天早上遇见她，下午我就有了第一次同性体验。所以，那真是值得纪念的一天。晚上我和她一起去保龄球，那天下午我却和另一个男人在一起。

00:22:56.900 --> 00:23:12.570
<v Jerry Walters>So I thought well, the situation with the other man was scary and disappointing and painful, to say the least. And so I thought, well, fine. That's out of my system. I don't want any more of that.
<v Jerry Walters>所以我心想，好吧，和那个男人的事既可怕又令人失望，痛苦不堪，至少可以说。所以我心想，好吧，没事了。我已经把它排除出我的生活了。我不想再那样了。

00:23:12.570 --> 00:23:30.140
<v Ira Glass>He went on to marry the girl, who he's still married to nearly three decades later. But soon after their wedding, he became increasingly obsessed with men. He found himself driving out to the forest preserves, where gay men were known to hang out. Men would walk over to his car. Men would try to talk to him.
<v Ira Glass>他继续娶了那个女孩，并将近三十年后依然维持着婚姻。但在婚礼后不久，他变得越来越痴迷于男性。他发现自己开车去森林保护区，那里是众所周知的同性恋聚集地。男人们会走到他的车边，男人们会试图和他搭讪。

00:23:30.140 --> 00:23:58.950
<v Jerry Walters>And I would drive out of the forest preserve areas like a bat out of hell, to be perfectly honest. And thinking that, fine, I didn't do anything. So there's no reason for me to feel guilty. But I would end up with headaches that were so severe that I couldn't work. I would come home four or five days a week, and just be incapacitated with the pain. But then I was taking aspirins and Tylenol, and everything else. I got hooked on tranquilizers. And it's a scary thing.
<v Jerry Walters>老实说，我会像躲避瘟神一样飞快地从森林保护区开车逃离。心里想着，没关系，我什么都没做。所以我没理由感到内疚。但最后我总是会头痛剧烈到无法工作。我每周有四五天回到家时，因疼痛而失去行动能力。那时候我服用阿司匹林、泰诺还有其他各种药。我对镇静剂上瘾了。那真是件可怕的事。

00:23:58.950 --> 00:24:38.470
<v Jerry Walters>When this obsession first takes hold, where first you are thinking about it, and before you know it, you are completely consumed by the thought of doing something with another male. You're not even sure of what you want to do. You're not sure who you want to do it with or where these people are, but you are totally consumed with that quest. Well suddenly, my wife became the person that was stopping me from pursuing what I absolutely, positively had to do to survive. And your thinking gets distorted, would be an understatement.
<v Jerry Walters>当这种执念第一次扎根时，起初你只是在思考它，不知不觉中，你完全被这种和另一个男性做点什么的念头所消耗。你甚至不确定你想做什么。你不确定你想和谁做，也不确定这些人都在哪里，但你完全被那种追求所消耗。突然间，我的妻子成了那个阻碍我追求为了生存绝对必须要做的事情的人。说你的思维变得扭曲，那都算轻的。

00:24:38.470 --> 00:24:43.110
<v Ira Glass>Why what happens? What do you start to think about?
<v Ira Glass>为什么？发生了什么？你开始想些什么？

00:24:43.110 --> 00:25:13.510
<v Jerry Walters>Maybe I shouldn't say this and maybe it's unique in my situation, but you think, gee, if she had an accident or something-- yes, I knew-- it sounds bizarre. But you're almost ready to plot to kill somebody. And I've told her this. And it's something that we-- here's a very mild mannered person, almost plotting somebody's demise, because they're stopping you. And they don't even know what's going on at this point.
<v Jerry Walters>也许我不该说这些，也许这在我的处境中很特殊，但你会想，天哪，如果她出了什么意外之类的……是的，我知道，这听起来很古怪。但你几乎准备策划杀人。我告诉过她这一点。而且这……这是一个非常温和的人，几乎在策划谋杀某人，因为他们在阻碍你。而当时他们甚至不知道发生了什么。

00:25:13.510 --> 00:25:27.180
<v Ira Glass>Do you think that at that time, before you told your wife, do think that in a day-to-day way, you had a lot of resentment that you would act out on, you would snap at her, that you would just be short with her? Because what you are describing is being so resentful.
<v Ira Glass>你认为在那个时候，在你告诉妻子之前，你是否觉得在日常生活中，你有很多怨恨会表现出来？你会对她发脾气，你会对她很不耐烦吗？因为你所描述的正是那种强烈的怨恨。

00:25:27.180 --> 00:25:42.110
<v Jerry Walters>No, I didn't. I held it inside. You see, this was your wife. You can't do this. I'm one of these people that holds the door open for women and very courteous with people. So I held it inside. And you think the top of your head is going to blow off instead.
<v Jerry Walters>不，我没有。我把它藏在心里。你看，这是你的妻子。你不能那样做。我是那种会为女性扶门，对人非常有礼貌的人。所以我把它藏在心里。你只会觉得自己的头顶快要炸开了。

00:25:42.110 --> 00:25:47.870
<v Ira Glass>Finally, after two years of marriage, he got up the courage to tell his wife he wanted to have sex with men.
<v Ira Glass>最终，结婚两年后，他鼓起勇气告诉妻子他想和男人发生性关系。

00:25:47.870 --> 00:26:25.450
<v Jerry Walters>She says, "Oh, is that all?" And I said, "You don't understand. I've got these feelings for men and I don't know how to deal with them." And she says, "Well, I married all of you. I didn't marry part of you. We'll just figure out how to deal with it." And so from there, we just set the guidelines that would work for us. I asked her what she needed to be comfortable? She wanted, number one, to know where I would be. And that's fine. I would sooner leave a phone number underneath the telephone, that if I don't show up by 10 o'clock or 12 o'clock, she calls.
<v Jerry Walters>她说：“噢，就这些？”我说：“你不明白。我有这些对男人的感觉，我不知道该怎么处理。”她说：“嗯，我嫁的是完整的你，不是一部分的你。我们会找出处理它的方法。”所以从那时起，我们就设定了对我们有效的准则。我问她，她需要什么才能感到舒服？她说，第一，想知道我会去哪里。那很好。我宁愿把电话号码留在电话下面，如果我10点或12点还没回去，她就打电话。

00:26:25.450 --> 00:26:49.730
<v Jerry Walters>She wanted me to be home when the kids got home, or when the kids woke up in the morning, which is fine. The term, "are you sleeping with somebody," I think, is kind of stupid. If I'm going someplace to sleep, I'll sleep at home. I'm going out for sex, not for sleeping. So that was never a problem. That's really the only things. And she said, "I want to be the most important person in your life." And she always has been and she always will be.
<v Jerry Walters>她希望孩子们放学回家时，或者孩子们早上醒来时，我能在家里，这很好。“你有没有和别人睡觉”这个词，我觉得挺傻的。如果我要去什么地方睡觉，我会回家睡。我出去是为了性，不是为了睡觉。所以那从来不是问题。真的就是这些。她说：“我想成为你生命中最重要的人。”她一直都是，也永远会是。

00:26:49.730 --> 00:27:00.610
<v Ira Glass>He says, of course, he uses condoms. So he doesn't being home any infections or AIDS. His children and the people he works with don't know he's gay, though after his father died, he told his mother.
<v Ira Glass>他说，当然，他使用避孕套。所以他不会把任何感染或艾滋病带回家。他的孩子和他一起工作的人不知道他是同性恋，尽管他父亲去世后，他告诉了他的母亲。

00:27:00.610 --> 00:27:02.760
<v Ira Glass>Why stay married?
<v Ira Glass>为什么要维持婚姻？

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:45.630
<v Jerry Walters>Because I love her and she loves me. And we're probably the best thing that happened to each other, ever. See, I don't like-- there's a difference between gay feelings and living the gay lifestyle, a dramatic difference. And I know a lot of people will disagree with this and maybe take offense at this. There is an arrested adolescence in the gay community. There is an acceptance of lying because it was needed to survive. And honesty is something that I really put a high price on and I really value it.
<v Jerry Walters>因为我爱她，她也爱我。我们可能是彼此生命中发生过的最好的事。看，我不喜欢……同性恋感受和过同性恋生活方式之间是有区别的，巨大的区别。我知道很多人会不同意这一点，也许会为此感到被冒犯。同性恋社区里有一种被捕的青少年心态。有一种对谎言的接受，因为那是生存所必需的。而诚实是我真正看重并给予很高价值的东西。

00:27:45.630 --> 00:28:07.050
<v Ira Glass>I suggest to him that he's the one who lies, by staying in a straight marriage, and not telling his own children he's gay. He says he's not really lying to his children. As he explains it, there's no easy time to sit your kids down and tell them that you're gay. When they're 5 or 10, it'll make no sense. When they hit adolescence, it could be confusing, a kind of burden, as they sort through their own sexual identities.
<v Ira Glass>我向他指出，正是他自己通过维持一段异性恋婚姻，并且不告诉自己的孩子他是同性恋，而在撒谎。他说他并没有真的对孩子们撒谎。照他的解释，没有一个简单的时间点可以坐下来告诉孩子们你是同性恋。当他们5岁或10岁时，这毫无意义。当他们进入青春期时，这可能会令人困惑，成为一种负担，因为他们正在整理自己的性认同。

00:28:07.050 --> 00:28:14.120
<v Ira Glass>If his children ask him directly he says, then he'll tell the truth. And he says, they'll ask when they're ready to hear the answer.
<v Ira Glass>他说，如果他的孩子们直接问他，他会说实话。他说，当他们准备好听到答案时，他们自然会问。

00:28:14.120 --> 00:28:22.940
<v Jerry Walters>If you want to know something, you will ask the question. If you don't ask the question, either you know the answer or you don't want to deal with the answer. Am I right?
<v Jerry Walters>如果你想知道某件事，你会提问。如果你不提问，要么是你已经知道答案，要么是你不想面对答案。对吗？

00:28:22.940 --> 00:28:25.050
<v Ira Glass>How old are your children?
<v Ira Glass>你的孩子们多大了？

00:28:25.050 --> 00:28:27.420
<v Jerry Walters>They're in their 20's. They're girls.
<v Jerry Walters>她们二十多岁了，是两个女儿。

00:28:27.420 --> 00:28:33.990
<v Ira Glass>Hm. Well, what is your assessment of what's going on? Do you think that they know, but they choose not to ask consciously?
<v Ira Glass>嗯，那么，你如何评价现在发生的一切？你觉得她们知情，但为了刻意回避而选择不去询问吗？

00:28:33.990 --> 00:28:34.390
<v Jerry Walters>Yes.
<v Jerry Walters>是的。

00:28:34.390 --> 00:28:35.390
<v Ira Glass>And it's a conscious choice?
<v Ira Glass>那是一个有意识的选择吗？

00:28:35.390 --> 00:28:43.730
<v Jerry Walters>Yes. I think it's very much a conscious choice. I think that they accept me for who I am. And I don't think they want to know a whole lot more about it.
<v Jerry Walters>是的，我认为这绝对是有意识的选择。我觉得她们接受真实的我，而且我不认为她们想知道太多关于这方面的事。

00:28:43.730 --> 00:28:50.810
<v Ira Glass>But you're saying they accept you for who you are, but they don't actually know entirely who you are, because you keep a certain part from them.
<v Ira Glass>但你说她们接受真实的我，可她们并不完全了解你是谁，因为你对她们隐瞒了一部分。

00:28:50.810 --> 00:29:30.610
<v Jerry Walters>I am who my children see. The only thing different about me is that I have sex with men. That is the only difference. If that makes me a different person in their eyes, what value is that? How is that going to enhance me as a father if suddenly this is in the equation too? I'm a good father. I'm a good husband. I take care of them. I was there when they needed things, and with school and growing up and advice and everything. And I don't think that what a person does in their bedroom, or someone else's bedroom, really is your children's concern.
<v Jerry Walters>我的孩子们看到的就是真实的我。我和别人唯一的不同就是我和男人发生性关系，这才是唯一的区别。如果这在她们眼中让我变成了一个不同的人，那这有什么价值呢？如果突然之间把这个因素加进来，这怎么会提升我作为父亲的形象呢？我是一个好父亲，一个好丈夫。我照顾她们。当她们需要帮助，在学校生活、成长和寻求建议时，我一直都在。我不认为一个人在自己的卧室——或者别人的卧室——里做什么，真正需要由你的孩子们来操心。

00:29:30.610 --> 00:29:56.970
<v Ira Glass>Later in our conversation, Jerry says that he'd like to tell his daughters the truth, but his wife doesn't want him to. She doesn't want anybody to know. He feels he has to respect her wishes. I ask him if his wife is simply ashamed that other people will know her husband looks for sex outside of the marriage? No, says Jerry. She knows the marriage works for them and simply doesn't want to have to put up with the opinions of people who won't understand. And they do have a sex life, Jerry says, of a sort.
<v Ira Glass>在稍后的对话中，杰瑞说他想告诉女儿们真相，但他妻子不让他这么做。她不想让任何人知道。他觉得他必须尊重妻子的意愿。我问他，他妻子是不是仅仅因为担心别人知道她丈夫在婚外寻求性生活而感到羞耻？杰瑞说不。她知道这段婚姻对他们来说是奏效的，她只是不想应付那些无法理解的人的议论。杰瑞说，他们确实有某种形式的性生活。

00:29:56.970 --> 00:30:00.020
<v Ira Glass>Are you as sexually attracted to women as you are to men?
<v Ira Glass>你对女性的性吸引力和对男性一样强烈吗？

00:30:00.020 --> 00:30:24.790
<v Jerry Walters>No. We don't depend on each other for our sexual satisfaction. I don't know if I should go into that or not. Probably not. Masturbation is something that is part of what we do for sexual satisfaction. And however you do it, it's satisfying.
<v Jerry Walters>不。我们不会依赖对方来满足性需求。我不知道我是否应该深入谈论这个。可能不应该。手淫是我们获取性满足的一部分。不管你怎么做，这都是满足的。

00:30:24.790 --> 00:30:26.230
<v Ira Glass>Does she see other men?
<v Ira Glass>她会和别的男人在一起吗？

00:30:26.230 --> 00:30:30.760
<v Jerry Walters>No. She says that's not what I'm about.
<v Jerry Walters>不。她说这不是她的风格。

00:30:30.760 --> 00:30:52.430
<v Ira Glass>We talked for two hours. It started to get cold in the car. Over time, as we talked, it became clear that this man stayed in his marriage, partly because he couldn't imagine any other life for himself. To Jerry, being an adult means having a wife, a house in the burbs, a couple kids, dinner parties, and mortgage payments, and mowing the lawn.
<v Ira Glass>我们谈了两个小时，车里开始冷了。随着谈话的深入，我很清楚，这个男人留在婚姻中，部分原因是他无法想象自己还有别的生活。对杰瑞来说，成年就意味着有妻子、郊区的房子、几个孩子、晚餐聚会、房贷和修剪草坪。

00:30:52.430 --> 00:31:05.310
<v Jerry Walters>That was the only way that people lived. I mean anything else wasn't even considered. And so how could I be anything other than what I am? And that was to be a married man.
<v Jerry Walters>那是人们唯一的生活方式。我的意思是，其他任何方式根本不在考虑范围内。所以我怎么可能变成除我之外的其他样子呢？那就是做一个已婚男人。

00:31:05.310 --> 00:31:11.480
<v Ira Glass>Do you think that you could have a kind of relationship like you have with your wife, with another man?
<v Ira Glass>你觉得你能和另一个男人建立那种和你妻子一样的关系吗？

00:31:11.480 --> 00:31:12.760
<v Jerry Walters>No.
<v Jerry Walters>不能。

00:31:12.760 --> 00:31:18.830
<v Ira Glass>I tell him that these kind of long-term, marriage-like relationships are commonplace among the gay men I know.
<v Ira Glass>我告诉他，这类长期的、类似婚姻的关系在我认识的男同性恋者中很常见。

00:31:18.830 --> 00:31:31.140
<v Jerry Walters>In the suburbs, it's not commonplace. Let's face it. In the city it's accepted. There are areas in the city where men can hold hands, where men can show affection outwardly. You won't find that in the suburbs.
<v Jerry Walters>在郊区，这不常见。让我们面对现实吧。在城市里，这被接受。城市里有一些区域，男人可以手牵手，可以公开表达爱意。你在郊区是找不到这些的。

00:31:31.140 --> 00:31:37.640
<v Ira Glass>But I find there's a part of you which doesn't even believe that it exists.
<v Ira Glass>但我发现你内心深处甚至不相信那种关系的存在。

00:31:37.640 --> 00:31:39.750
<v Jerry Walters>It doesn't, where I live.
<v Jerry Walters>在我住的地方，它确实不存在。

00:31:39.750 --> 00:31:58.770
<v Ira Glass>For a while, I told him about Susan Bergman's book, about how difficult it was for her when she realized that her father wasn't what he seemed. I suggested to Jerry that his own daughters might feel betrayed, might find it hard to trust him if someday they find out he's been less than candid about his sexuality. He didn't agree.
<v Ira Glass>有一段时间，我跟他讲了苏珊·伯格曼的书，讲到当她发现父亲并非她所看到的那样时，她内心是多么痛苦。我向杰瑞暗示，如果有一天他的女儿们发现他对自己性取向的问题一直不够坦诚，她们可能会感到被背叛，可能会觉得难以再信任他。他不同意。

00:31:58.770 --> 00:32:28.960
<v Jerry Walters>Shall we take the Easter Bunny and Santa Claus in that context too, that your parents told you that this man is Santa Claus? Do you hate your parents or distrust your parents' judgment from that point on? When you were a child, you were given just enough information to live your life. They didn't tell you that the landlord's going to throw you out of the house. They didn't tell you that your father's going to lose his job. They didn't tell you a lot of the things that could hurt you, because they were your parents. Their job was to protect you.
<v Jerry Walters>那我们是不是也该把复活节兔和圣诞老人的事放在这个语境下讨论？你父母告诉你这世上有圣诞老人。从那时起，你就恨你的父母或者不信任他们的判断了吗？当你还是个孩子时，你只得到了足够让你活下去的信息。他们没告诉你房东会把你赶出去。他们没告诉你你父亲会失业。他们没告诉你很多可能会伤害你的事情，因为他们是你的父母。他们的工作是保护你。

00:32:28.960 --> 00:32:35.530
<v Ira Glass>Her situation is that her father was, on the surface anyway, presented the image of being a family man, a very religious man--
<v Ira Glass>她的情况是，她父亲表面上呈现出的形象是一个顾家的男人，一个非常虔诚的男人——

00:32:35.530 --> 00:32:35.890
<v Jerry Walters>Right.
<v Jerry Walters>没错。

00:32:35.890 --> 00:32:42.010
<v Ira Glass>--was a musician in their church. And so there was this constant lie, because--
<v Ira Glass>——是教会里的音乐家。所以这就存在一个持续的谎言，因为——

00:32:42.010 --> 00:33:09.920
<v Jerry Walters>Where is the lie? Was he not all of those things? Wasn't he all of those things, plus one other thing? We are complicated individuals. We can be a lot of things. You said he was a musician. He was a family man. He was a minister. He was a lot of things. And he was just this one more thing, that he didn't choose to share with her. And I think that was his privilege.
<v Jerry Walters>谎言在哪里？他难道不是那些身份吗？他不仅是那些身份，还多了一样东西，不是吗？我们是复杂的个体。我们可以有很多面。你说他是音乐家，他是顾家男人，他是牧师，他是很多东西。而他只是多了一样他不想与她分享的东西。我觉得那是他的特权。

00:33:09.920 --> 00:34:45.650
<v Ira Glass>How much do you need to know a person to love them, to live with them? Jerry says he talks to about 260 men a year in the support group he leads for gay and bisexual married men. He says he urges men to think very seriously about what they'll be giving up if they choose to quit their marriages, if they choose to be honest with their families, if they choose to end their double lives.
<v Ira Glass>你需要多了解一个人才能爱他，才能和他生活在一起？杰瑞说，在他领导的已婚男同性恋和双性恋支持小组中，他每年会接触大约260人。他说他敦促男人们认真思考，如果他们选择结束婚姻，如果选择对家人诚实，如果选择结束双重生活，他们将会失去什么。

00:34:45.650 --> 00:36:02.250
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, lies that every parent tells, including a secret that my mom has kept from her kids. And yes, it involves sex. Yes. In a minute, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，每个父母都会说的谎言，包括我妈妈对我隐瞒的一个秘密。是的，它和性有关。是的。稍后，节目继续。

00:36:02.250 --> 00:36:21.050
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, invite a variety of writers and artists to take a whack at that theme with monologues, documentaries, short fiction, short radio plays, anything we can think of. Today's program, Double Lives. Act Three, Lighten Up.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周我们的节目都会设定一个主题，邀请各种作家和艺术家围绕这个主题进行创作，形式包括独白、纪录片、短篇小说、短广播剧，任何我们能想到的形式。今天的节目主题是“双重生活”。第三幕，放轻松。

00:36:21.050 --> 00:36:58.550
<v Ira Glass>Up until now, we've been hearing about pretty serious lies, told between parents and children. But there are lots of trivial lies that parents tell. In fact, you can reasonably argue that it is impossible to raise children without lying to them, which brings us to the Neo-Futurists. The Neo-Futurists are a Chicago group who perform 30 plays in 60 minutes every Friday and Saturday night. And they prepared this play, they call it a play. I think non-Neo-Futurists would call it a sketch. They prepared this play on our theme today. It's by Greg Allen, a recent father himself.
<v Ira Glass>到目前为止，我们听到了关于父母和孩子之间非常严重的谎言。但父母也会说很多无关紧要的谎言。事实上，你可以合理地认为，不欺骗孩子就无法抚养他们，这让我们想到了“新未来主义者”。“新未来主义者”是芝加哥的一个团体，他们每周五和周六晚上会在60分钟内表演30出戏。他们准备了这出戏——我想称之为戏。我想非“新未来主义者”会称之为小品。他们针对我们今天的主题准备了这出戏。作者是格雷格·艾伦，他本人也是一位刚当上爸爸的人。

00:36:58.550 --> 00:37:03.540
<v Greg Allen>Twenty-one lies I will tell my children. Go. Number one.
<v Greg Allen>我会对我的孩子们说的二十一个谎言。开始。第一条。

00:37:03.540 --> 00:37:07.300
<v Woman>If you don't get down, you're going to break your neck.
<v Woman>如果你不下来，你会摔断脖子的。

00:37:07.300 --> 00:37:08.110
<v Greg Allen>Two.
<v Greg Allen>第二条。

00:37:08.110 --> 00:37:09.560
<v Woman>Santa's coming.
<v Woman>圣诞老人要来了。

00:37:09.560 --> 00:37:10.470
<v Greg Allen>Three.
<v Greg Allen>第三条。

00:37:10.470 --> 00:37:14.270
<v Woman>Mommy was just, um, singing. Go back to bed.
<v Woman>妈妈刚才只是，嗯，在唱歌。回去睡觉吧。

00:37:14.270 --> 00:37:14.760
<v Greg Allen>Four.
<v Greg Allen>第四条。

00:37:14.760 --> 00:37:18.230
<v Man>I will never forget this for as long as I live.
<v Man>我这辈子都不会忘记这件事的。

00:37:18.230 --> 00:37:18.820
<v Greg Allen>Five.
<v Greg Allen>第五条。

00:37:18.820 --> 00:37:20.860
<v Woman>I'm sure he didn't mean to hurt you.
<v Woman>我相信他不是故意伤害你的。

00:37:20.860 --> 00:37:21.650
<v Greg Allen>Six.
<v Greg Allen>第六条。

00:37:21.650 --> 00:37:24.140
<v Woman>I said shucks. Oh, shucks.
<v Woman>我说的是“该死”。噢，该死。

00:37:24.140 --> 00:37:24.990
<v Greg Allen>Seven.
<v Greg Allen>第七条。

00:37:24.990 --> 00:37:29.080
<v Woman>Sure. I like your music. It's just different.
<v Woman>当然，我喜欢你的音乐。只是风格不同而已。

00:37:29.080 --> 00:37:29.610
<v Greg Allen>Eight.
<v Greg Allen>第八条。

00:37:29.610 --> 00:37:33.020
<v Woman>One more time and you're going to get out and walk.
<v Woman>再有一次你就给我下车走回去。

00:37:33.020 --> 00:37:33.750
<v Greg Allen>Nine.
<v Greg Allen>第九条。

00:37:33.750 --> 00:37:38.040
<v Man>Just because your friends do it, doesn't mean you have to do it.
<v Man>别人家的孩子都这么做，不代表你也得这么做。

00:37:38.040 --> 00:37:38.940
<v Greg Allen>Ten.
<v Greg Allen>第十条。

00:37:38.940 --> 00:37:41.900
<v Woman>Oh now, you don't really mean that.
<v Woman>哦，别这样，你不是真心的。

00:37:41.900 --> 00:37:42.890
<v Greg Allen>Eleven.
<v Greg Allen>第十一条。

00:37:42.890 --> 00:37:47.450
<v Woman>Sure. I like your haircut. It's just different.
<v Woman>当然，我喜欢你的发型。只是风格不同而已。

00:37:47.450 --> 00:37:48.300
<v Greg Allen>Twelve.
<v Greg Allen>第十二条。

00:37:48.300 --> 00:37:50.040
<v Woman>I never said that.
<v Woman>我没说过那种话。

00:37:50.040 --> 00:37:50.950
<v Greg Allen>Thirteen.
<v Greg Allen>第十三条。

00:37:50.950 --> 00:37:53.440
<v Man>No one is going to notice a little pimple.
<v Man>没人在意长个小痘痘的。

00:37:53.440 --> 00:37:54.230
<v Greg Allen>Fourteen.
<v Greg Allen>第十四条。

00:37:54.230 --> 00:37:57.400
<v Woman>Sex is the expression of love and devotion.
<v Woman>性是爱与奉献的表达。

00:37:57.400 --> 00:37:58.220
<v Greg Allen>Fifteen.
<v Greg Allen>第十五条。

00:37:58.220 --> 00:38:02.930
<v Woman>It doesn't matter about the new car. All that matters is that you're OK.
<v Woman>新车怎么样不重要，重要的是你没事就好。

00:38:02.930 --> 00:38:03.720
<v Greg Allen>Sixteen.
<v Greg Allen>第十六条。

00:38:03.720 --> 00:38:06.030
<v Man>I'm going to kill you.
<v Man>我要杀了你。

00:38:06.030 --> 00:38:07.360
<v Greg Allen>Seventeen.
<v Greg Allen>第十七条。

00:38:07.360 --> 00:38:12.100
<v Woman>Sure. I like your boyfriend. He's just different.
<v Woman>当然，我喜欢你的男朋友。他只是有点不同。

00:38:12.100 --> 00:38:12.880
<v Greg Allen>Eighteen.
<v Greg Allen>第十八条。

00:38:12.880 --> 00:38:15.570
<v Woman>I was just closing my eyes for a minute.
<v Woman>我只是闭眼休息了一小会儿。

00:38:15.570 --> 00:38:16.400
<v Greg Allen>Nineteen.
<v Greg Allen>第十九条。

00:38:16.400 --> 00:38:19.330
<v Woman>You were the best baby in the world.
<v Woman>你是世界上最乖的宝宝。

00:38:19.330 --> 00:38:20.430
<v Greg Allen>Twenty.
<v Greg Allen>第二十条。

00:38:20.430 --> 00:38:23.350
<v Woman>Everything is going to be all right.
<v Woman>一切都会好起来的。

00:38:23.350 --> 00:38:24.370
<v Greg Allen>Twenty-one.
<v Greg Allen>第二十一条。

00:38:24.370 --> 00:38:28.150
<v Man>Daddy will always be here to take care of you.
<v Man>爸爸永远会在这里照顾你。

00:38:28.150 --> 00:38:29.960
<v Greg Allen>Curtain.
<v Greg Allen>谢幕。

00:38:29.960 --> 00:38:37.640
<v Ira Glass>Greg Allen, Diana Slickman, Dave Awl, Heather Riordan, David Kodeski, Anita Loomis, and Stephanie Shaw, of the Neo-Futurists.
<v Ira Glass>格雷格·艾伦、戴安娜·斯利克曼、戴夫·奥尔、希瑟·里奥丹、大卫·科德斯基、安妮塔·卢米斯和斯蒂芬妮·肖，“新未来主义者”成员。

00:38:37.640 --> 00:39:22.260
<v Ira Glass>Our parents can surprise us with what they don't tell us, with what they don't talk about, especially when it comes to sex. Recently, I had this experience. An ex-girlfriend was in the gym, looking through a copy of a Marie Claire magazine, a woman's magazine. And there was an article in it on women's fantasies, their sexual fantasies, "What Do Your Man's Dirty Daydreams Reveal About What He Wants from You?" In the article, six sexperts-- that was the word they used, sexperts-- reveal the six most common male sex fantasy scenarios. So my ex-girlfriend is reading. And there, in the third paragraph, one of the sexperts turns out to be my mother.
<v Ira Glass>父母那些不告诉我们的事，那些他们闭口不谈的事，尤其是涉及到性的时候，常常让我们感到惊讶。最近，我就有过这样的经历。一位前女友在健身房看一本叫《嘉人》的女性杂志。里面有一篇文章讨论女性的幻想，她们的性幻想——“你男人的那些肮脏白日梦揭示了他对你的什么渴望？”在文章中，六位“性学专家”——他们是这么称呼的——揭示了男性最常见的六种性幻想场景。我前女友正在读。就在第三段，其中一位性学专家竟然是我母亲。

00:39:22.260 --> 00:39:27.320
<v Ira Glass>[PHONE RINGING]
<v Ira Glass>[电话铃声]

00:39:27.320 --> 00:39:28.050
<v Shirley Glass>Hello.
<v Shirley Glass>喂。

00:39:28.050 --> 00:39:28.570
<v Ira Glass>Hey, Mom.
<v Ira Glass>嘿，妈。

00:39:28.570 --> 00:39:29.120
<v Shirley Glass>Yeah.
<v Shirley Glass>嗯。

00:39:29.120 --> 00:39:30.170
<v Ira Glass>It's Ira.
<v Ira Glass>是艾拉。

00:39:30.170 --> 00:39:31.160
<v Shirley Glass>Yeah.
<v Shirley Glass>嗯。

00:39:31.160 --> 00:39:32.460
<v Ira Glass>So I'd like to do a little interview.
<v Ira Glass>我想做一个小采访。

00:39:32.460 --> 00:39:33.350
<v Shirley Glass>OK.
<v Shirley Glass>好的。

00:39:33.350 --> 00:39:38.690
<v Ira Glass>OK. So Mom, can I read to you a quote from an article?
<v Ira Glass>好的。妈，我可以读一段文章里的引语给你听吗？

00:39:38.690 --> 00:39:39.500
<v Shirley Glass>Of course.
<v Shirley Glass>当然可以。

00:39:39.500 --> 00:39:55.680
<v Ira Glass>OK. Here it is. "Your man wants a woman who excites him through her own excitement. You could stimulate yourself while he watches or let him participate by moving his hand to where you want it."
<v Ira Glass>好。在这里。“你的男人想要一个能通过自身兴奋来让他兴奋的女人。你可以在他观看时进行自我刺激，或者引导他，把他的手放到你想要他放的地方。”

00:39:55.680 --> 00:39:57.230
<v Shirley Glass>Yeah.
<v Shirley Glass>嗯。

00:39:57.230 --> 00:40:00.350
<v Ira Glass>That's you being quoted in Marie Claire.
<v Ira Glass>这就是你在《嘉人》杂志里被引用的内容。

00:40:00.350 --> 00:40:06.420
<v Shirley Glass>[LAUGHTER] You're kidding. What issue?
<v Shirley Glass>[笑] 你在开玩笑吧。哪一期的？

00:40:06.420 --> 00:40:29.980
<v Ira Glass>All I know is that Anaheed was at the gym. And she opens up Marie Claire to an article called, "Men's Sexual Fantasies." And it says at the top here, "Sexperts reveal the six most common scenarios, unlock the secret longings and psyches of the modern men who fantasize." And you, basically, are one of this sexperts.
<v Ira Glass>我只知道阿纳希德在健身房，她翻开了《嘉人》杂志，看到了一篇题为“男人的性幻想”的文章。顶部写着：“性学专家揭示了六种最常见的场景，解开那些有幻想的现代男人们的秘密渴望和心理。”而你，基本上就是这些性学专家之一。

00:40:29.980 --> 00:40:31.850
<v Shirley Glass>Yeah. Yeah, I am.
<v Shirley Glass>是啊，确实是我。

00:40:31.850 --> 00:40:35.520
<v Ira Glass>I didn't really know you were a sexpert?
<v Ira Glass>我真的不知道你是性学专家？

00:40:35.520 --> 00:40:37.360
<v Shirley Glass>What did you think I was?
<v Shirley Glass>那你以为我是什么？

00:40:37.360 --> 00:40:43.380
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHTER]. Just another Jewish mom and psychologist.
