<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Sins of the Fathers. What does it do to children if parents tell a big lie for years? Part of Susan Bergman's book, Anonymity, is her trying to understand the two parts of her father, strict, religious, family man; promiscuous, gay, night clubber. His personality was split in half, she writes, into two irreconcilable halves.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，父辈的罪孽。如果父母多年来撒下弥天大谎，会对孩子造成什么影响？苏珊·伯格曼的书《匿名》的一部分，是她试图理解她父亲的两面性：严谨、虔诚的顾家男人；滥交、同性恋、夜店常客。她写道，他的性格一分为二，变成了两个不可调和的部分。

00:49:05.600 --> 00:49:27.420
<v Ira Glass>But part of her book is about her discovery that growing up in his home, she became somebody who was also split in half, somebody able to carry on a life with two irreconcilable parts. She found herself as an adult, leading a double life. To end our program, we asked her to read from her book.
<v Ira Glass>但她书中的一部分是关于她的发现：在那种家庭中长大，她也变成了一个性格分裂的人，一个能够维持两种不可调和生活部分的人。成年后的她发现自己过着双重生活。在节目的最后，我们请她朗读了她的书。

00:49:27.420 --> 00:49:56.450
<v Susan Bergman>I pretend I am a faithful wife. My husband is married to that faithful woman. The woman looks like me. She moves around in my body. This is what I mean. You can't tell by looking. He makes love to her. He has asked her a question once or twice, and she has heard herself reply with an avoidance, "Is there anything you haven't told me? I want to know more of you, Susan."
<v Susan Bergman>我假装自己是一位忠诚的妻子。我的丈夫娶了那个忠诚的女人。那个女人长得像我。她在我的身体里活动。这就是我的意思。你光看是看不出来的。他与她做爱。他曾一两次问过她一个问题，而她听到自己含糊其辞地回答：“有什么你还没告诉我的吗？我想更多地了解你，苏珊。”

00:49:56.450 --> 00:50:33.600
<v Susan Bergman>They spent last evening on opposite ends of the house, keeping things going, changing the music, refreshing people's drinks and trays of food. "What makes you ask that tonight after such a great party? All our friends here, scrumptious food. Didn't you enjoy yourself?" She unfastens her gold-beaded bracelet and folds it carefully into its silk-lined box, replacing the lid. "I saw the way Tom looked at you." "There's absolutely nothing for you to worry about between me and anyone here tonight. Most definitely nothing's up with Tom."
<v Susan Bergman>他们昨晚在房子的两端度过，维持着局面，换音乐，招待客人，补充饮料和盘中的食物。“在这么棒的派对之后，你怎么会问这个？我们所有的朋友都在，食物美味。你没玩得开心吗？”她解开金珠手链，小心翼翼地把它折叠进丝绸衬里的盒子里，盖上盖子。“我看到汤姆看你的眼神了。”“我和今晚在场的任何人之间绝对没什么可担心的。汤姆那儿绝对什么事也没有。”

00:50:33.600 --> 00:51:05.520
<v Susan Bergman>She concentrates on slowing down, as she hangs up her belt and tosses her stockings in a basket of hand washing. "You're doing this jealous thing again. What kind of look?" "Do you have any secrets from me?" "A few." She'll keep it light. "This is not my natural hair color, quite. But the rest is real."
<v Susan Bergman>她集中精神放慢动作，挂起腰带，把长袜扔进洗涤篮里。“你又在胡乱吃醋了。什么样的眼神？”“你有事瞒着我吗？”“有一点。”她会轻松地带过。“这不是我天生的发色。但剩下的都是真的。”

00:51:05.520 --> 00:51:45.460
<v Susan Bergman>You must understand that lying is a temporal invisibility. It's the leaves you wrap yourself in when the voice in the garden calls. I was learning to deflect any doubt or question about my faithfulness, back onto the questioner, so that I didn't have to perpetuate the lie. I had for years, part lied, but mostly told the truth. Two and more irreconcilable parts, which let me understand my father, or made me into him. Here was my father's ailment again, his dread of being known.
<v Susan Bergman>你必须明白，撒谎是一种暂时的隐身。当园子里的声音呼唤你时，谎言就是你用来包裹自己的叶子。我当时正在学习如何把对他人的怀疑或关于我忠诚度的质疑挡回去，转而抛向提问者，这样我就不必继续维持这个谎言了。多年来，我部分在撒谎，但大部分时间是在说实话。这两个互不相容的部分让我理解了我的父亲，或者说，把我变成了他。这就是我父亲的顽疾再次显现：他害怕被别人了解。

00:51:45.460 --> 00:52:41.570
<v Susan Bergman>There's a family with children on the line. I force my family to serve as the same kind of false front I was raised to be from my father. Our presence testified to his normality. We failed, no matter how we strove for blessing, to discover the root of our calamity. I can't shake his choice alone, I tell myself. So we slipped and fell, which is human. And he died, stuck. And this gluey lie I keep perpetuating, sticks to me like a curse, revisited on the next generation. My father is in the window when I glance up, and in the hurried tone of my voice, in the shape of my ribs. What if, lights on, as is, he had asked us to love him?
<v Susan Bergman>有一家人带着孩子在排队。我强迫我的家人扮演和我父亲当年要求我扮演的一样的虚假门面。我们的存在证明了他的正常。无论我们如何努力寻求祝福，我们都没能发现我们灾难的根源。我告诉自己，我无法独自摆脱他的选择。于是我们滑倒并坠落，这是人之常情。而他死的时候，依然深陷其中。这种我一直在延续的黏糊糊的谎言，像诅咒一样粘在我身上，又在下一代身上重演。当我抬头看时，我父亲就在窗边，我的说话语调变得仓促，连我的肋骨形状都像他。如果当初灯火通明，维持原状，他直接要求我们爱他，会怎样呢？

00:52:41.570 --> 00:53:28.700
<v Susan Bergman>I opened the book I had slipped into my jacket pocket and read, "Lord, who shall abide in thy tabernacle? Who shall dwell on thy holy hill? He that walketh uprightly, and worketh righteousness, and speaketh the truth in his heart. Who shall ascend into the hill of the Lord? Or who shall stand in his holy place? He that hath clean hands, and a pure heart; who hath not lifted up his soul unto vanity; nor sworn deceitfully." If her hands are clean and her heart pure, I had been forgiven. But I had yet to go to the man I had wronged, and whom I desperately wanted to love me, to cancel my ongoing lie, my maintaining the family pattern of dishonesty.
